## Pelvic floor exercises

For **stress incontinence**, NICE recommends a course of **supervised pelvic floor muscle training** for at least **3 months**. These exercises strengthen the muscles that support your bladder. The NHS **Squeezy** app can remind you to do them.

## Bladder training

For **urge incontinence**, **bladder training** for at least 6 weeks can help. You learn to wait longer between trips to the toilet.

## Everyday changes

- drink normal amounts of fluid. Drinking less can make things worse
- cut down on **caffeine**, fizzy drinks and alcohol
- lose weight if you need to
- treat constipation

## Medicines

For overactive bladder, medicines such as **solifenacin** or **mirabegron** can help. Vaginal oestrogen may help after the menopause.

## Procedures

If other treatments have not worked, you may be referred to a specialist. Options include **botox injections** into the bladder for overactive bladder, or surgery for stress incontinence. The use of vaginal mesh for stress incontinence has been restricted in the UK since 2018, and you should get full information about the risks of any procedure.

## Continence products

Pads, pants and other products can help while you wait for treatment to work. Your local **NHS continence service** may provide them if you meet their criteria.
